The aarch64 virtio keyboard driver back-converts Linux input event codes to
PS/2 set-1 scancodes (plain below 0x49, extended via ext_key_map with the
0xE0 prefix).  Three common keys were missing from ext_key_map and were
dropped with "virtio: unmapped keycode N" (measured in the 2.3.2 release,
2026-11-18): Insert (110), Delete (111) and Keypad Enter (96).  mini-commander
under the graphical Terminal never sees them, because the Terminal translates
kernel key events to ANSI sequences for the pty.

The target scancodes are exactly the ones lib/kbd.c already maps:
0x52 -> KEY_INSERT, 0x53 -> KEY_DEL, 0x1c -> KEY_NUM_ENTER (key '\n').

--- a/kernel/arch/aarch64/virtio.c
+++ b/kernel/arch/aarch64/virtio.c
@@ -290,6 +290,9 @@ static const uint8_t ext_key_map[256] = {
 	[0x6a] = 0x4D, /* RIGHT */
 	[0x6b] = 0x4F, /* end */
 	[0x6d] = 0x51, /* page down */
+	[0x60] = 0x1c, /* keypad enter (kbd.c: KEY_NUM_ENTER, '\n') */
+	[0x6e] = 0x52, /* insert     (kbd.c: KEY_INSERT) */
+	[0x6f] = 0x53, /* delete     (kbd.c: KEY_DEL) */
 	[0x7d] = 0x5b, /* left super */
 };
